<p class="lead">Two LED fixtures can draw the same wattage and put out the same amount of light, yet make a brick home look inviting or oddly clinical. The difference is color temperature. Choosing the right shade of white is one of the most important decisions in a landscape lighting design, and one of the easiest to get wrong when fixtures are bought off a shelf.</p>

<h2>Kelvin, explained simply</h2>
<p>Color temperature describes how warm or cool white light appears, measured in Kelvin (K). Lower numbers look warmer, more amber, closer to candlelight or an old incandescent bulb. Higher numbers look cooler and bluer, closer to midday sky.</p>
<p>The scale feels backwards to most people, so it helps to remember one anchor: the old halogen landscape lights that many Spring homes grew up with sit at roughly 2700K to 3000K. That warm glow is what most of us picture when we think of a home lit at night.</p>

<h2>The common options</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>Color temperature</th><th>Appearance</th><th>Where it tends to work</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>2200K</td><td>Deep amber, candle-like</td><td>Patios, fire features, historic or rustic homes, wildlife-sensitive areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>2700K</td><td>Warm white, cozy</td><td>Red brick, warm stone, wood, entries and seating areas</td></tr>
<tr><td>3000K</td><td>Soft warm white, slightly crisper</td><td>Most facades, limestone, mixed materials, general landscape use</td></tr>
<tr><td>4000K</td><td>Neutral to cool white</td><td>Selected foliage, blue-green plants, modern architecture, used sparingly</td></tr>
<tr><td>5000K</td><td>Cool, bluish daylight</td><td>Rarely right for residential landscapes; reads as commercial or security lighting</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>For most homes in North Houston, 2700K and 3000K do the bulk of the work. Spring Landscape Lighting typically treats 3000K as a starting point and adjusts from there based on the materials on site.</p>

<h2>Matching color to materials</h2>
<p>Light color does not exist in isolation. It interacts with whatever it hits, and the right choice depends on the surface.</p>

<div class="grid-cards">
<div class="mini-card"><h3>Red and orange brick</h3><p>Warm light at 2700K brings out the red and makes brick feel rich. Cool light can turn it muddy or pinkish gray.</p></div>
<div class="mini-card"><h3>Limestone and cream stone</h3><p>3000K keeps limestone clean and creamy without turning it yellow. 2700K can push pale stone toward gold, which some owners love.</p></div>
<div class="mini-card"><h3>Austin stone and ledgestone</h3><p>These buff and tan stones look best at 2700K to 3000K, especially when grazed so the texture reads clearly.</p></div>
<div class="mini-card"><h3>Stucco and painted surfaces</h3><p>Match the paint. Warm whites and earth tones suit 2700K to 3000K. Crisp white or gray modern homes can carry 3000K or a touch cooler.</p></div>
</div>

<h3>Plants and foliage</h3>
<p>Here the conversation gets more interesting. Warm light makes green leaves look slightly yellow or olive, which is fine for most residential settings and keeps the whole scene cohesive. Cooler light around 4000K makes greens look fresher and more saturated. It can work well on blue-green plants such as agave, Texas sage or other silvery foliage, and occasionally on dense green canopies viewed from a distance.</p>
<p>That said, most live oaks, magnolias and crape myrtles in Spring look natural and welcoming at 3000K. Reach for cooler light deliberately, not by default.</p>

<h3>Water</h3>
<p>Water features and pools often look clearer with slightly cooler light, 3000K to 4000K, because it emphasizes the blue of the water. Warm light can make water look tea-colored, especially if it is not perfectly clean.</p>

<p>To see these combinations side by side, use the interactive guide below or open the full <a href="/tools/color-temperature-guide/">color temperature guide</a>.</p>
