<h2>Why CRI matters too</h2>
<p>Color Rendering Index (CRI) measures how accurately a light source shows colors compared with natural light, on a scale up to 100. Cheap LEDs with a low CRI, around 70 to 80, can make brick look flat and foliage look gray even at the right color temperature. For landscape work, a CRI of 80 is a reasonable minimum and 90 or higher is noticeably better on red brick, flowering plants and skin tones on a patio.</p>
<p>Two fixtures labeled 3000K can look different if one has a much higher CRI. That is one reason professional-grade fixtures tend to look richer than budget ones.</p>

<h2>Mixing temperatures (carefully)</h2>
<p>Mixing color temperatures can work, but only on purpose. A common, effective approach is warm light on the house and hardscape (2700K or 3000K) with slightly cooler light on select foliage. The key is separation: the cooler fixtures should light plants that sit apart from the facade, so viewers do not see two whites side by side on the same plane.</p>
<div class="callout callout--warn"><strong>Avoid:</strong> Mixing 2700K and 4000K on the same wall or the same tree. The eye picks up the mismatch immediately and the property starts to look patched together.</div>

<h2>Consistency across fixtures</h2>
<p>A system built over time from different brands is where color problems often appear. One fixture's 3000K is not always another's. LEDs also shift slightly as they age, and replacement lamps bought years later may not match. A few practical habits help:</p>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Stick to one fixture family where possible.</strong> Same manufacturer, same LED source, same color temperature.</li>
<li><strong>Buy spares at installation.</strong> A few matching lamps on the shelf make future replacements seamless.</li>
<li><strong>Replace in groups.</strong> If one old lamp fails in a run of six, consider replacing the set so they age together.</li>
<li><strong>Check at night.</strong> Color differences that are invisible in a showroom become obvious on a dark facade.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you are upgrading an older system, our guide to <a href="/halogen-to-led-landscape-lighting/">converting halogen to LED</a> covers how to match the warm look homeowners are used to.</p>

<h2>RGBW and tunable white</h2>
<p>Color-changing RGBW fixtures and tunable white fixtures let you adjust color from an app. Tunable white is genuinely useful: you can set a facade at 2700K most of the year and nudge foliage cooler in summer. Full RGB color is fun for holidays, game days or a pool party, but most homeowners find they use it occasionally and keep warm white the rest of the time. These fixtures cost more and need compatible <a href="/services/smart-lighting-controls/">smart lighting controls</a>, so they are best reserved for areas where flexibility really matters, such as the pool or a feature tree.</p>

